Trust is a fragile, cumulative good — organisations, governments, and individuals that violate ethical constraints 'for good reasons' erode the trust that makes cooperation possible, eventually undermining the goals they sought.
Whistleblowing law, professional ethics codes, and constitutional rights are all designed to constrain 'ends justify means' reasoning at institutional level, because experience shows where unconstrained reasoning leads.
Rawls' veil of ignorance thought experiment shows that rational people designing rules without knowing their social position would choose robust rights protections over utilitarian calculations — revealing that ethical constraints are not merely sentimental.
